---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CopyBlock from '../../components/CopyBlock.astro';

const REPO = 'https://github.com/thirdspaced/talktorobotsstyleguide';

const library = await getCollection('library');
const core = library.find((e) => e.id === 'llm-style-guide');
const corePrompt = core?.data.prompt ?? '';

interface Edition {
  name: string;
  page: string;
  file: string;
  bestFor: string[];
  adds: string;
}

const editions: Edition[] = [
  {
    name: 'Core',
    page: '/library/llm-style-guide/',
    file: '/style-guide/llm-style-guide-v3.md',
    bestFor: ['Emails and messages', 'Social posts and newsletters', 'Bios, web pages, and instructions'],
    adds: 'The full rule set for everyday writing, with general writing modes and examples.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Business',
    page: '/library/llm-style-guide-business/',
    file: '/style-guide/llm-style-guide-v3-business.md',
    bestFor: ['LinkedIn and professional accounts', 'Website, service copy, and case studies', 'Proposals, client email, and listings'],
    adds: 'Startup language to rewrite, business claims that need proof, and calls to action that serve the reader.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Tech',
    page: '/library/llm-style-guide-tech/',
    file: '/style-guide/llm-style-guide-v3-tech.md',
    bestFor: ['Explainers and news roundups', 'Tool reviews and release notes', 'Setup docs and security notices'],
    adds: 'Rules for news, AI features, and security copy, plus a tech hype table.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Education',
    page: '/library/tone-and-voice-style-guide-for-educators/',
    file: '/style-guide/llm-style-guide-v3-education.md',
    bestFor: ['Lesson materials and directions', 'Student feedback', 'Family newsletters and staff messages'],
    adds: 'School terms, three school audiences, and rules against inventing student observations or progress.',
  },
];

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'The LLM style and copy guide, version 3',
    description:
      'Free writing rules you give to ChatGPT, Claude, or Gemini so AI drafts sound like a person wrote them. Core, business, tech, and education editions.',
    author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Amanda Grutza' },
    datePublished: '2026-10-26',
    license: 'https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="LLM style and copy guide, version 3 · how to talk to robots"
  description="Free writing rules you give to ChatGPT, Claude, or Gemini so AI drafts sound like a person wrote them. Core, business, tech, and education editions, open source on GitHub."
  schema={schema}
>
  <article class="prose section">
    <p class="kicker">Style guide · Version 3</p>
    <h1>The LLM style and copy guide</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      A free set of writing rules you give to ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, or any other AI chatbot.
      Upload it once, and the drafts that come back use plain words, keep your facts, and drop
      the habits that make people stop reading.
    </p>
    <p class="cta-row">
      <a class="button" href="#editions">Pick an edition</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href={REPO} rel="noopener">See it on GitHub <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
    </p>

    <h2>What it's for</h2>
    <p>
      Ask an AI tool for a post or an email and the first draft usually shows the same habits:
      em dashes everywhere, "delve" and "seamless," a cheerful intro, three tidy bullets, a
      recap of what you just read, and sometimes a number nobody measured. Readers notice, even
      when they can't say what's off.
    </p>
    <p>
      The guide tells the model which words and sentence patterns to avoid, what to write
      instead, and how to run a six-check final pass before it hands the draft back. It's just as
      strict about what to keep: the facts, the limits, the exact names and button labels, and
      the next step your reader needs.
    </p>

    <div class="before-after" aria-label="Before and after example">
      <figure class="card">
        <figcaption>Before</figcaption>
        <p>"AI isn't just a tool. It's your unfair advantage. The people who embrace it today will be the ones leading tomorrow. Let that sink in."</p>
      </figure>
      <figure class="card after">
        <figcaption>After the style pass</figcaption>
        <p>"Before adding AI to your week, pick the one task you want to change. Drafting a reply from meeting notes is a specific task. 'Be more productive' is too broad to test. Try it for a week and notice how much editing the drafts still need."</p>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <p>
      It's an editing standard. It isn't an AI detector, and its word lists aren't proof that
      someone used AI. Plenty of people wrote "delve" long before chatbots did.
    </p>

    <h2>Who can use it</h2>
    <p>
      Anyone who uses an AI tool to draft something they'll send under their own name or their
      organization's name. You don't need to be technical. If you can upload a file to a chat,
      you can use it. It was written with these readers in mind:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Small business owners, freelancers, and anyone writing for a professional account (<a href="/library/llm-style-guide-business/">business edition</a>)</li>
      <li>People who write about technology: newsletters, reviews, release notes, docs, security notices (<a href="/library/llm-style-guide-tech/">tech edition</a>)</li>
      <li>Teachers and school staff writing for students, families, and colleagues (<a href="/library/tone-and-voice-style-guide-for-educators/">education edition</a>)</li>
      <li>Everyone else who wants their email or post to sound like them (<a href="/library/llm-style-guide/">core edition</a>)</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="editions-heading" id="editions">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2 id="editions-heading">Pick your edition</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="prose-note">
      Every edition is complete on its own. Choose the one closest to what you write most and
      upload that single file.
    </p>
    <div class="grid-editions">
      {editions.map((e) => (
        <article class="card guide-card edition">
          <p class="kicker">Version 3</p>
          <h3><a href={e.page}>{e.name}</a></h3>
          <ul>
            {e.bestFor.map((b) => <li>{b}</li>)}
          </ul>
          <p>{e.adds}</p>
          <p class="edition-links">
            <a class="card-link" href={e.page}>Read it</a>
            <a class="chip" href={e.file} download>Download .md</a>
          </p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="prose section">
    <h2>How to use it</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <h3>Fill in Make it yours</h3>
        <p>
          The table near the top of each edition sets whose voice it is, who reads it, three words
          for how you want to sound, and the words you never want to see. Anything you leave blank
          falls back to the defaults.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Upload it to a project</h3>
        <p>
          Add the file to a Claude Project, a ChatGPT Project or custom GPT, or a Gemini Gem. If
          your tool doesn't have projects, paste it into custom instructions or at the start of a
          chat.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Start each task with the instruction</h3>
        <p>
          Every edition ends with a starter instruction. Fill in the brackets and paste it in with
          your notes. Here's the core version:
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <CopyBlock text={corePrompt.trim()} label="Copy the starter instruction" />
    <ol class="steps" start="4" style="counter-reset: step 3;">
      <li>
        <h3>Read the draft yourself</h3>
        <p>
          The final pass catches patterns. It can't check your facts, your dates, or whether the
          story in the draft really happened. That part stays with you.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What's in every edition</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A Make it yours table for your voice, audience, and house terms</li>
      <li>Rules for behavior, thinking, mechanics, openings, and closings</li>
      <li>Words and sentence patterns to avoid, with plain replacements</li>
      <li>Evidence rules: no invented numbers, quotes, testing, or results</li>
      <li>Cadence, tone, and format notes for the places you publish</li>
      <li>Worked before-and-after examples</li>
      <li>A six-check final pass and a starter instruction</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Each file also starts with skill frontmatter, so you can drop it into a folder, rename it
      <code>SKILL.md</code>, and use it as a Claude skill.
    </p>

    <h2>Version history</h2>
    <dl class="versions">
      <dt>Version 3, October 2026</dt>
      <dd>
        Four editions (core, business, tech, education), the Make it yours table, format notes for
        each place you publish, stricter evidence rules, expanded phrase tables, and new worked
        examples.
      </dd>
      <dt>Version 2, July 2026</dt>
      <dd>
        Sentence-pattern bans, rules against reflexive praise and "I hope this helps," evidence
        rules, a longer vocabulary list, and the six-check final pass.
      </dd>
    </dl>
    <p>The full list of changes is in the <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md`} rel="noopener">changelog</a>.</p>

    <h2>Free and open source</h2>
    <p>
      The guide is MIT licensed. Copy it, adapt it, and put your name on your version. If you
      find a pattern it misses, or a rule that gets in the way of good writing,
      <a href={`${REPO}/issues`} rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a> or
      <a href="/contribute/">send it in here</a> with an example and a rewrite. A preference
      that's only yours belongs in your own Make it yours table.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .kicker {
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--brick);
    margin: 0 0 0.35rem;
  }
  .lead {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .cta-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin: 1.25rem 0 0.5rem;
  }
  .prose-note {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin: 0 0 1.1rem;
  }
  .before-after {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16rem, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0 1.25rem;
  }
  .before-after figure {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .before-after figcaption {
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }
  .before-after p {
    font-size: var(--text-base, 1rem);
    margin: 0;
  }
  .before-after .after {
    border-top: 3px solid var(--stamp-green);
  }
  .before-after .after figcaption {
    color: var(--stamp-green);
  }
  .grid-editions {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(14.5rem, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
  }
  .edition p:not(.kicker):not(.edition-links) {
    flex: 1;
  }
  .edition-links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin: 0.5rem 0 0 !important;
  }
  .edition-links .card-link {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  .versions {
    margin: 0.75rem 0 1rem;
  }
  .versions dt {
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }
  .versions dd {
    margin: 0.25rem 0 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
</style>
